WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.650
Narrator: If you want to
get to the opposite end

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:06.310
of the world, it's a hike.
About 20,000 kilometers.

00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:09.460
But what if you didn't have
to travel across the surface?

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:12.733
What if you could dig straight
through to the other side?

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:17.000
If you're trying to dig
to China from the US,

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:19.020
there's something you should know first.

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:22.440
The opposite point on the
planet isn't in China.

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:25.610
It's somewhere in the
middle of the Indian Ocean.

00:00:25.610 --> 00:00:27.660
So, to get to China,

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:30.360
you should start digging in
either Argentina or Chile.

00:00:31.270 --> 00:00:32.600
Your first challenge would be

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:34.560
digging through the Earth's crust.

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:37.510
It's the thinnest of
Earth's three main layers,

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:41.500
yet humans have never drilled
all the way through it.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:43.870
As you descend, you'd soon reach the depth

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:47.270
of the Paris Catacombs,
the deepest metro station,

00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:48.340
and the devil worm,

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:51.290
the deepest animal we've
ever discovered underground.

00:00:51.290 --> 00:00:53.600
Then, it would start to get hot.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:57.220
At 4,000 meters down,
you'd pass the deepest mine

00:00:57.220 --> 00:00:59.580
on the planet, which is cooled with ice

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:02.760
to make workers comfortable,
because, down here,

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:05.800
temperatures are 60 degrees Celsius.

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:10.790
By 8,800 meters, you'll be as
deep as Mt. Everest is tall,

00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:14.420
but it's still not the deepest
point humans have ever dug.

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:17.790
That point is at the bottom of
the Kola Superdeep Borehole,

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:21.260
at 12,260 meters below the surface.

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:24.000
Down here, there's 4,000
times more pressure

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.483
than at sea level,

00:01:25.483 --> 00:01:29.041
and temperatures push 180 degrees Celsius,

00:01:29.041 --> 00:01:31.210
so you'd need a lot of insulation

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:33.950
to carry on and keep from melting.

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:37.500
At around 40,000 meters,
you'd reach Earth's second

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:40.180
and largest layer, the mantle,

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:44.580
which makes up a whopping
84% of the planet's volume.

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:45.630
Near the border,

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:49.330
temperatures climb to around
1,000 degrees Celsius,

00:01:49.330 --> 00:01:51.270
hot enough to melt many metals,

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:54.460
like silver, but not a steel drill.

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:56.210
And good thing because you'll need it

00:01:56.210 --> 00:01:58.060
to drill through the
first part of the mantle,

00:01:58.060 --> 00:01:59.900
which is made of solid rock,

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:02.370
until you reach 100,000 meters, that is,

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:05.020
when you might need to
switch to a propeller.

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:08.090
Here, the pressure and
temperature are so high that,

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:12.560
in some places, rock takes on
a caramel-like consistency.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:15.610
In fact, it's this rock
that ultimately erupts

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:17.760
from volcanoes on the surface.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:21.790
At 150,000 meters, keep your
eyes peeled for diamonds.

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:23.160
They form when heat

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:26.440
and pressure restructure the
carbon atoms in this region.

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:30.360
Once you reach 410,000 meters,
the rock is solid again,

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:32.470
so it's back to the drill.

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:34.680
You see, while it's still plenty hot

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:36.450
at this depth to melt rock,

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:39.620
the pressure is so
extreme that the molecules

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:42.890
inside literally can't
move into a liquid state.

00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:45.000
Then, by 3 million meters down,

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:48.580
you'd reach Earth's third
layer, the outer core.

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:51.150
Unlike Earth's crust and mantle,

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:53.910
the core is made of iron and nickel.

00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:57.910
Temperatures here are the same
as the surface of the sun,

00:02:57.910 --> 00:03:00.290
hot enough to melt all that metal,

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:02.980
so, yep, back to the propeller.

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:03.950
And it would have to be made

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:06.290
out of some kind of supermaterial,

00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:08.400
because no known element
has a melting point

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:10.860
above 6,000 degrees Celsius.

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:15.050
Making matters worse, the outer
core also has low gravity,

00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:16.720
because, when you're that deep,

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:19.540
much of the planet's
mass is now above you,

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:21.410
which produces a gravitational force

00:03:21.410 --> 00:03:23.530
that pulls away from the center.

00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:25.320
So to continue,

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:28.470
you'd need a super heat-
and pressure-proof submarine

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:30.540
that moves like rockets in space

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:33.120
by shooting fuel out the back end.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:35.300
You'd soon arrive at the inner core,

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:38.400
around 5 million meters below the surface.

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:41.550
The inner core is one
giant sphere of solid iron,

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:45.030
so it would definitely be
challenging to get through.

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:46.810
But if you did find a way,

00:03:46.810 --> 00:03:48.920
you'd soon hit the halfway point,

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:51.610
about 6.4 million meters down,

00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:54.810
also known as the center of the Earth.

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:58.100
Now, there's nearly the same
amount of mass all around you,

00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:00.520
pulling you equally in all directions,

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:02.600
so there's zero gravity here.

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:06.800
And now is when the trip really
gets hard. The second half.

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:09.610
Because as you dig past the inner core,

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:12.100
you'd soon feel the pull of gravity again.

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:15.610
And this time, it'd be
pulling you from above,

00:04:15.610 --> 00:04:18.530
where the majority of Earth's mass is now.

00:04:18.530 --> 00:04:20.620
So while you might be digging down,

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:22.190
relative to where you started,

00:04:22.190 --> 00:04:24.370
it'll feel like you're climbing up.

00:04:24.370 --> 00:04:27.540
And if you didn't have those
handy rockets propelling you,

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:30.570
you'd fall right back to the core.

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:33.570
But 6.4 million meters later,

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:36.290
after powering through impenetrable iron,

00:04:36.290 --> 00:04:39.800
molten alloy, and solid and mushy rock,

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:44.800
you'd arrive, at long last,
on the other side, in China.

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:46.740
That would certainly come as a relief,

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:49.450
but it wouldn't even be the best part.

00:04:49.450 --> 00:04:51.680
Assuming you left a tunnel
through the center of the Earth

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:55.140
behind you, you'd now be
able to travel back and forth

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:58.460
between China and
Argentina in under an hour,

00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:00.640
simply by jumping in.

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:03.100
To learn why, check out
another video we made

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:06.241
about jumping through
the center of the Earth.

